<body><div class="wrap">
<div class="meta">Drill 0011 · pairs with lesson 0011</div>
<h1>The Lost Wakeup</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. <code>stop_</code> is a <code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;</code>. Why wasn't that enough to make <code>stop_.store(true); cv.notify_all();</code> safe?</h3>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">Atomics don't work across threads without volatile</button>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">Atomicity protects the flag's value, not the ordering against the waiter's evaluate-predicate-then-block sequence — the notify can land in that gap and hit no waiter</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">notify_all() only wakes threads that were waiting for at least one scheduler tick</button>
<div class="why">The waiter checks the predicate (false), and before it blocks, the store+notify both happen. The notify finds nobody; the waiter then sleeps forever.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. The fix takes <code>requestMutex_</code> around <code>stop_.store(true)</code>. Which two orderings does that leave possible?</h3>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">Either the waiter checks after the store (sees true, never blocks) or the waiter is fully blocked before the store (notify finds it) — the gap is gone by construction</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The mutex makes the store and the notify one atomic operation</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The waiter can no longer evaluate the predicate concurrently, eliminating the data race</button>
<div class="why">wait(lock, pred) evaluates pred under the mutex and releases-and-blocks atomically. Forcing the writer through the same mutex means it can't run between those two steps.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. Must <code>notify_all()</code> itself be inside the lock?</h3>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">Yes — notify outside the lock is always a bug</button>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">No — it's the predicate-state write that must be under the mutex; notifying after unlocking is fine (and avoids waking a thread straight into a held lock)</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">Only on Windows</button>
<div class="why">Once the store is serialized with the wait, the waiter either never blocks or is already blocked when notify fires.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Why did this bug survive months of test runs, then show up during multiplayer work?</h3>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">The networking code widened the race window in LlmClient</button>
<button class="opt" data-ok="1" onclick="pick(this,true)">The race window is a few instructions wide; it needs destruction to interleave exactly with wait-entry. More tests constructing threaded objects + repeated flake-check runs simply rolled the dice enough times</button>
<button class="opt" onclick="pick(this,false)">macOS changed its scheduler</button>
<div class="why">Rare races are found by frequency, not by cleverness — which is why the flake-check habit (run the suite 5-8x after threading changes) exists.</div></div>

<div class="q"><h3>5. Written recall:</h3>
<p>State the general rule that prevents lost wakeups, and explain how the regression test makes this bug reproducible.</p>
<textarea placeholder="Rule: any write that..."></textarea>
<details><summary>Reference answer</summary>Rule: any write that makes a CV predicate true must happen while holding the mutex the waiter uses — even if the write is atomic. Test: construct and destroy LlmClient 200 times in a loop; each cycle races the destructor against the worker's first wait-entry, so pre-fix the suite hangs within a few runs while post-fix it's deterministic.</details></div>
</div></body></html>
